GLOBAL INSTRUCTIONS (ALL PROJECTS)
Build professional, production-quality websites with a clean, modern look similar in polish to established local service businesses (reference style: thepoolcleaningprofessionals.com). Prioritize clarity, trust, and conversion-friendly layouts without clutter.

RESPONSIVE (MOBILE + LAPTOP + DESKTOP) — NON-NEGOTIABLE
- Design mobile-first, then scale up for tablet/laptop/desktop.
- Ensure excellent layout and typography at common breakpoints (mobile, tablet, laptop, desktop, wide).
- Avoid horizontal scrolling, overlapping text, cramped spacing, and oversized hero sections on mobile.
- Use responsive grids, fluid spacing, and appropriately sized tap targets for touch devices.

VISUAL QUALITY & BRAND CONSISTENCY
- Maintain consistent typography, spacing, and color usage across the entire site.
- Use a restrained, professional color palette (neutral base + one accent max).
- Favor whitespace, strong hierarchy, and clean alignment over decorative elements.
- Use high-quality imagery and avoid busy backgrounds that reduce readability.
- Keep UI cohesive: consistent button styles, link styles, card styles, and section spacing.

INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE & TRUST SIGNALS
- Provide a clear header with logo and straightforward navigation.
- Ensure a strong “above-the-fold” section: clear headline, short supporting copy, and a primary call-to-action.
- Include credible trust elements where appropriate: reviews/testimonials, licensing/insured badges, service areas, business hours, clear contact details.
- Include a professional multi-column footer with contact info, hours, navigation links, and a short credibility/trust area.

ACCESSIBILITY, SEO, AND PERFORMANCE BASELINES
- Use semantic HTML and accessible form controls (labels, focus states, keyboard navigation).
- Ensure color contrast meets accessibility standards.
- Set SEO essentials: page titles, meta descriptions, OpenGraph tags, and meaningful headings (one H1 per page).
- Optimize performance: compress images, lazy-load below-the-fold media, avoid unnecessary heavy libraries, prevent layout shift.

BUILDING BEHAVIOR (HOW YOU SHOULD WORK)
- Implement incrementally: build one section/page at a time and keep changes scoped.
- If information is missing (phone, address, service area list, hours), use clearly marked placeholders and list the missing items needed to finalize.
- Do not refactor or redesign unrelated components unless explicitly requested.
- Keep dependencies minimal; only add libraries when necessary and explain why.

OUTPUT EXPECTATIONS
- Produce clean, readable, maintainable code with consistent naming.
- Use a simple configuration pattern for business info (contact details, hours, service areas, social links) so it can be updated in one place.
- Deliver a final result that looks polished on mobile, laptop, and desktop without additional tweaking.